                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire. Balliol
                        College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘On 55 is a pencil scribble (14th
                        cent.?): Ad’ de Rideborn’ aule Wynt’ | Petrus framl. (lined through and
                        Clark written over) in eadem aula | T. Warte (?) in aula depehall | . . .
                        snathin (?) aule chemyne | J. Feryby depehall | Reg’ Lexham aul’ Christofori
                        (these––Chimney Hall, Deep Hall, etc.––are academic halls of residence in
                        medieval Oxford).’ (Mynors, Balliol, 88) ‘On 1v [. . .] an erased
                        ownership-mark, perhaps Liber communitatis scolarium aule de . . . pro anima
                        magistri Johannis D . . . [. . .] Langbaine says ‘Donum magistri Johannis
                        Donkes quondam socii’ (even Emden i. 586 knows nothing of him), and Balliolo
                        and Donkys are possible supplements; though if this is a College
                        inscription, one would not expect to find it erased, unless the volume was
                        at some date alienated. At the top of 2 is the late 14th-cent. Balliol
                        inventory-mark 139 commentator philosophie pret. xis (the numeral written
                        over an erasure)’ (Mynors, Balliol, 88)</p></provenance>
